Note: You may need to adjust some of these Assertions based on your specific ArrayList usage and what you expect the results to be. These are just some general assumptions that were made when creating this sample.

And finally, here's a quick overview of the [PexObserve.ValueForViewing](https://research.microsoft.com/en-us/um/cambridge/projects/sfts/ms-plt-pecore-generation-and-verification.aspx) method call and how you might use it:

- The **PexObserve.ValueForViewing** method is for adding additional information to be displayed on the Visual Studio Test Explorer UI.
- This is particularly helpful for understanding the behavior under varying inputs.
- Information added to this method will indeed be displayed on the Test Explorer UI for failed test runs.
- It is quite similar to Console.WriteLine, but a bit more user-friendly.
- If you are working with arrays or collections, it's better to use PexObserve.ValueForViewing with non-primitive types (non-structs) to avoid serialization issues.
- As a note of caution: **PexObserve.ValueForViewing** should not be used for testing, but for debugging purposes only. The values added there are merely helpful for the users but will not be part of the test assertions.
- There is no limit on the quantity or frequency of values you can use this for. It's mostly there for the convenience of seeing what a value is when a test fails, rather than to cause tests to fail when the value is arbitrary (unlike Assert.AreEqual).
  
Happy Testing!!

